{
  "requirement": "Number of hexadecimal digits in the identity hash of an anonymous selector segment.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub const HASH_DIGITS: usize = 12;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "`requirements.sdk.outlines.HASH_DIGITS` declares one public constant, `pub const HASH_DIGITS: usize = 12`. The constant gives the number of hex digits in an anonymous selector segment's identity hash. The only obligations are that the value is 12 and that anonymous segments carry that many hex digits.\n\n- **Value:** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs#fn[unknown_extensions_keep_nonblank_lines_and_content_selectors]` asserts `assert_eq!(HASH_DIGITS, 12)`. It uses the constant imported from `outline::selector`.\n- **Use in anonymous segments:** The same test checks every anonymous `line@…` segment in a plain-text outline. It strips the `line@` prefix and asserts the length equals `HASH_DIGITS`. It asserts every character is an ASCII hex digit. It asserts the prefix equals the first 12 characters of `structural_hash`.\n- **Selector behaviour:** The test also asserts that identical lines get identical selectors and different lines get different ones. It asserts that `select` resolves such a selector, and that a reflowed source keeps the same selectors. This shows the hashed segments work as identity.\n- **Public export:** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` covers the constant's export path and item kind through the rustdoc inventory. That check is incidental to the value.\n\nI found no gap. The anonymous-segment construction sites in the tree-sitter and markdown outlines use the same constant. The requirement is about the constant and its documented value, so I don't require a separate per-language test of the digit count. I did not assess the implementation beyond locating where the constant is used.",
  "references": "626b887ea4829745118e76e022ba528f90dd6869e94e42a75be65619ddaab2e1",
